WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:02.135
<v Examiner>Four marks.

2
00:00:02.135 --> 00:00:06.683
<v Examiner>An older patient has become unsteady when mobilising to the bathroom.

3
00:00:06.683 --> 00:00:11.165
<v Examiner>Identify and describe five nursing strategies used to prevent falls.

4
00:00:11.165 --> 00:00:16.109
<v Examiner>Then explain how these strategies reduce harm and improve patient outcomes.

5
00:00:16.559 --> 00:00:21.387
This is Lisa's practice question with her marking guide, so you know the split:

6
00:00:21.387 --> 00:00:25.420
one mark for identifying five strategies, one for describing them,

7
00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:26.948
two for the explain part.

8
00:00:27.398 --> 00:00:30.019
Part one, five strategies, each described.

9
00:00:30.019 --> 00:00:32.765
Assess falls risk with the Morse Fall Scale.

10
00:00:32.765 --> 00:00:36.384
The score sets the level of care and shapes the care plan.

11
00:00:36.384 --> 00:00:41.938
Keep the environment free of hazards: a clear path from bed to bathroom, no trip hazards,

12
00:00:41.938 --> 00:00:42.812
good lighting.

13
00:00:42.812 --> 00:00:47.118
Call bell within reach, and teach the patient how and when to use it,

14
00:00:47.118 --> 00:00:50.301
so they can call for help instead of walking alone.

15
00:00:50.301 --> 00:00:51.923
Appropriate mobility aids:

16
00:00:51.923 --> 00:00:55.917
refer to the physio to review whether a walking frame is needed.

17
00:00:55.917 --> 00:01:01.284
Help with mobilising when needed, and non-slip socks, which grip better than slippers.

18
00:01:01.734 --> 00:01:02.840
Part two, explain.

19
00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:05.482
These strategies reduce the risk of injury,

20
00:01:05.482 --> 00:01:09.231
maintain the patient's independence and improve their safety.

21
00:01:09.231 --> 00:01:14.331
They prevent complications like fractures and head injuries, longer hospital stays,

22
00:01:14.331 --> 00:01:19.677
and the loss of confidence that keeps people from moving, which then makes them weaker.

23
00:01:19.677 --> 00:01:24.347
Falls are a leading cause of death in older adults, which is why it matters.

24
00:01:24.797 --> 00:01:27.756
The Morse Fall Scale, for the multi-choice.

25
00:01:27.756 --> 00:01:33.812
Six items: history of falling, secondary diagnosis, ambulatory aid, I V or heparin lock,

26
00:01:33.812 --> 00:01:35.464
gait, and mental status.

27
00:01:35.464 --> 00:01:38.905
Scores run from zero to a hundred and twenty-five.

28
00:01:38.905 --> 00:01:44.479
Zero is no risk, under twenty-five is low, twenty-five to forty-five is moderate,

29
00:01:44.479 --> 00:01:46.131
over forty-five is high.

30
00:01:46.581 --> 00:01:50.026
And if the question is wound assessment, use TIME.

31
00:01:50.026 --> 00:01:53.816
T, tissue: granulation, slough, necrotic or epithelial.

32
00:01:53.816 --> 00:01:57.536
I, inflammation or infection: pain, redness, swelling,

33
00:01:57.536 --> 00:02:00.155
and the look and smell of the exudate.

34
00:02:00.155 --> 00:02:04.220
M, moisture balance: dry, moist, wet, saturated or leaking.

35
00:02:04.220 --> 00:02:08.079
E, epidermal edges: is it closing, and its measurements.

36
00:02:08.079 --> 00:02:13.729
A holistic assessment covers physical, psychological, social and spiritual health,

37
00:02:13.729 --> 00:02:18.552
and the spiritual part includes values, meaning, concerns about death,

38
00:02:18.552 --> 00:02:20.275
and resuscitation status.

39
00:02:20.725 --> 00:02:25.852
Falls, Lisa's marking: one mark identify five, one describe, two explain.

40
00:02:25.852 --> 00:02:31.050
Assess with Morse; clear the environment; call bell in reach and teach it;

41
00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:35.264
mobility aids with physio; help to mobilise, non-slip socks.

42
00:02:35.264 --> 00:02:40.462
They reduce injury, keep independence, prevent fractures and longer stays.

43
00:02:40.462 --> 00:02:42.920
Morse over forty-five is high risk.

44
00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:47.485
Wounds: TIME, tissue, inflammation or infection, moisture, edges.
